#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/id #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8963" :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{3/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" :question/math-academy-id "e-8965" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{x^2}{y}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z =2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\frac{2}{x}, Q(x) = 2x^{2}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{∫(2/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{2}{x}z) &= x^{2} \\cdot (2x^{2}) \\\\\nx^{2} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} + 2xz &= 2x^{4} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2}) &= 2x^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2})dx &= ∫2x^{4}dx \\\\\nzx^{2} &= \\frac{2x^{5}}{5} + C_{1} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2x^{5} + C}{5x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1$.\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}$. This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n1. $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n2. $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n3. $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = e^x y^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = -xy^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95af464f-2ceb-5d1f-91dc-a0e739b124cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144017} {:answer/id #uuid "c5078068-9c21-57a9-b473-743568265bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144018} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7f7438-80ad-5b11-a14a-9dfccd12a646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144019} {:answer/id #uuid "019412bf-e5f1-502d-b0a1-434be097c290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144020} {:answer/id #uuid "14ec42ab-455a-524b-a1e9-21d5454eff03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95af464f-2ceb-5d1f-91dc-a0e739b124cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144017} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f65c53-793c-52f3-b8f5-55b470825532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144016}] :question/id #uuid "1480f672-8091-5903-a81e-b755f04694ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-106069" :question/problem "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + x^{2}y = xy^{4}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf424601-2419-5f27-8b90-c7eba796ebd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144023} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a1e672-cf1e-57d8-931b-78f5611dd482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144024} {:answer/id #uuid "120ae745-1855-5fff-97b4-5b9ad394a3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144025} {:answer/id #uuid "4682d7d3-fb20-5ef5-8d0d-69b9ce03ffd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144026} {:answer/id #uuid "77d4c96e-b5b5-54b2-a1bd-570ec2a8ae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d4c96e-b5b5-54b2-a1bd-570ec2a8ae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144027} :answer-field/id #uuid "b599fce9-e71e-5ffd-a98d-21b093ad0a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144022}] :question/id #uuid "b36fbf4d-bb56-53ea-bcd3-bd3fbb7b094e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106070" :question/problem "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{y}{x} = x\\sqrt{y}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca5db28-0418-5937-9e00-d2e889cf12ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2xe^{x}}{C - 2e^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144029} {:answer/id #uuid "d39baa47-f3dd-5b18-bff0-697818f50e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{e^{x}}{C - 2e^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144030} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a6d5c3-6a20-5e40-98a7-3fe6544e043a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2e^{x}}{C - e^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144031} {:answer/id #uuid "2de561a5-72b6-5a9a-a405-d798b9fe5a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C - e^{2x}}{2e^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144032} {:answer/id #uuid "75d8d6cf-b1b9-51d7-bbd0-f3146296a49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C - e^{x}}{2e^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3a6d5c3-6a20-5e40-98a7-3fe6544e043a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2e^{x}}{C - e^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144031} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d65a133-9570-530a-875f-040716d6574c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144028}] :question/id #uuid "ad742d4e-8bb3-5b2d-a572-286f9bbe2b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106073" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - y = y^{2}e^{x}$."}]}
